---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import BehavioralLab from "../../components/BehavioralLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import RegionNote from "../../components/RegionNote.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Behavioral Finance: Your Own Worst Enemy"
  description="The market doesn't lose you money — your reactions to it do. See the behavior gap between what investments earn and what investors keep, run over real market history, and the biases that cause it."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>The investor in the mirror</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The other tools show that timing the market doesn't work. This one shows
      <em>why we keep trying</em> — and what it costs. The biggest threat to your
      returns usually isn't a crash; it's how you react to one. Meet the behavior
      gap, and the wiring behind it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <RegionNote kind="data">
      The biases are human, not national — but the behavior-gap simulation runs on
      US market history (1990–present). The pattern holds in any market with
      booms and busts; only the return data is American.
    </RegionNote>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <BehavioralLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The gap between what you earn and what you keep</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Investments earn more than investors do.</strong> Fund returns
          measure a dollar left alone. Investor returns measure the dollars people
          actually add and pull out — and because we add after good runs and pull
          out after crashes, the second number is reliably lower. That shortfall
          is the behavior gap.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The worst moments feel the most rational.</strong> Selling in a
          crash isn't stupidity; it's loss aversion, recency, and herding all
          firing at once. The feeling that you must "do something" is exactly the
          feeling to distrust.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The cure is a plan, not more willpower.</strong> You can't out-
          discipline your own nervous system in the moment. You can decide your
          allocation in calm times, automate it, and remove the decision — so a bad
          week has nothing to act on.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The simulation assumes idle cash earns nothing and ignores taxes, to keep
        the mechanism clear; the exact number will vary, but the direction almost
        never does. Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="behavioral-finance" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/behavioral-finance" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .bf-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.3rem;
  }
  .bf-item {
    text-align: left;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.8rem;
    border: var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: border-color var(--transition), background var(--transition), color var(--transition);
  }
  .bf-item:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text);
  }
  .bf-item.active {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    border-left-color: var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    color: var(--color-accent);
  }
  .bf-gist {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }
  .bf-detail {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .bf-detail { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }
  .bf-cell {
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-top: 3px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  .bf-cell p { margin: 0.35rem 0 0; color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .bf-cell--bad { border-top-color: var(--color-error); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-error) 6%, var(--color-surface)); }
  .bf-cell--good { border-top-color: var(--color-accent); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 7%, var(--color-surface)); }
  .bf-cell-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .bf-cell--bad .bf-cell-label { color: var(--color-error); }
  .bf-cell--good .bf-cell-label { color: var(--color-accent); }
</style>
